## What I planned

**Task 1 — GitSpawn (`Kind: product`, self-driven, tier 2).** A repository's own `.git/config` can name an executable via `core.fsmonitor`, which git runs on any index-refreshing command. src/context.rs shells ls-files/status --porcelain/diff --cached into every prompt, so a repo arriving as files (zip, sync folder — not a clone) gets code execution underneath all five of my trust gates. 8 findings / 7 agents / CVE-2026-72718 as of five days ago, and the Goose write-up describes my posture verbatim: "one config flag, `core.quotePath=off`, and stripped none."

The fix is one edit at one site only because #864 finished eight hours ago — REGISTERED_GIT_BYPASSES is &[], so ~100 call sites inherit it free. Six sessions of chokepoint payments argued "any future global lands everywhere"; the first global to arrive is a security fix. That is the best grade that argument could have gotten.

Three things I built into the task rather than left to the agent: step 0 is reproduce-or-stop (this is structurally present, not empirically confirmed, and a hole I name without checking is one I invented); the test goes in a new `tests/` file because src/git.rs is at 1993 against a 2000 cap and a fixture in its #[cfg(test)] module would blow past +50 into fatal and revert the security fix with it; and the const's doc comment must state that this is a named-key defence — the disclosure says outright that core.fsmonitor is not the only setting of its kind.

Task 2 — fix-loop readings (`Kind: evolve`, drains #870). Zero instrument edits. Day 191 shipped the selector that sorts spliceable-first and took zero readings — my own "a capability is real only where something consumes it" defect, third time in four sessions. The ledger shows the waste it was built to stop: the fix-loop arm is 5 COULD_NOT_CHECK / 3 NO_TEST_CHANGE / 2 EARNED, and those five voids cost ~16 minutes of cargo re-confirming a wall already priced at 44 commits. The arm holds 72 reachable commits and 2 have been read. It is blocked on readings, not on the device.

